---
description: Checks and tests implement the design's full statement, not a necessary condition of it
alwaysApply: true
---

# Checking the design's statements

A check or a test that stands for a statement in `docs/design.ja.md` implements the whole statement, not a necessary condition of it.

- Every documented contract has a code path and a test. A sentence with no code behind it is a defect. A sentence of an XML doc is such a contract. PR #552's `HeadlessApp.Popups` said that the nodes in a popup are found with `Find`; `Find` walked the window's tree alone, no test called it on a popup, and P02d's second PR found it (PR #556).
- A rule about a press or a key is tested from each focus state the part can be in when the input comes: the part or an ancestor holding the focus, and another node holding it. P02b2's area started no selection on a part that keeps the focus only when the focus was elsewhere; with the area focused, the press left the focus on the area and started one, and the review found it (PR #503).
- The PR's table of statements and tests is made by copying the Issue's acceptance items one by one; an item that names a case ("to the leaf's end") has a test of that case before the PR is opened. P02b2's first PR listed the drawing's tests by the design's sentences, and the review found no test of a selection to the leaf's end or across an inline object's room, both named in Issue #499 (PR #500).
- Before the PR, list each contract sentence the PR adds to `docs/design.ja.md` next to the test that checks it. In S5 the review found four sentences the PR had written itself (one queue in order, a drain of the count at the call, the three cases of `Send`, a continuation that throws) with no test. P02a made no such list, and the review found three statements with no test: the order of `Find`'s keys, the exceptions of work on the UI queue, and the checks of the options (PR #461).
- An API that runs the caller's code (`Effect.Create`, an event handler, a message handler, a layout measure function) is tested with that code doing what it may do to the same subsystem: set a signal it or another reader reads, dispose itself, create another of its kind, throw. In S6b a measure function that threw lost the other layout requests of the pass; the review found it. In S6a the review, not a test, found that the first run of `Effect.Create` was not a batch.
- A walk up a tree stops at the root the tree names, not at the first node without a parent: nodes are built and styled before they are attached. In S6b every parentless node counted as the root, so a node styled before it was appended became a layout root and failed the pass; the review found it. A lookup of where a node is (its root, the popup it is in) made while the node is made finds it in no root: parts are made from the leaves up. A popup open when its anchor was made in a popup's content found no parent popup, opened as a popup of the window, and the parent, which opened next, closed it as a sibling; the popup now waits until its anchor joins a root, and a test makes it open from the start (PR #556).
- A rejection in `docs/design-decisions.md` whose reason is behavior ("separate queues lose the order between messages and continuations") has a test that fails when the rejected option is put in its place.
- When the design fixes an order (the frame lifecycle, the `ResizeBuffers` steps, the Scope disposal order, the error priority), test it with one input that fails two adjacent steps and assert the first.
- When the design fixes a state machine (ComputationStatus, ShowState, ResourceState), test every transition it lists and one transition it does not allow.
- A test of an invariant (§11.6) includes the input that would break it: a stale `NodeHandle`, a `ref` taken before an arena grows, a `Signal.Set` from a worker thread.
- A fixed-size buffer on a path every frame takes has a test above its capacity.
- A comparison removed from a key as the same as another is shown to have no input where the two differ, or it stays. In L4 the width a percentage margin reads was removed as equal to the row's main size, which it is not when the row's width is known but not definite; the review found the stale result (PR #103).
- When a statement lists the values a mechanism covers, each value has a test on a node where the other values' paths do not run. Design §2.4.1 says a parent marks a child when it reads a measurement or a baseline. In L1b the tests used only measured children, and a comment said the measurement had already marked the child; a child of fixed size is never measured, so reading its baseline left no mark, and the review found it (PR #91).
- A table in the design (the keys of a text field's commands, the values of an option) has a test for each row, and the PR's list of statements and tests has one line for each row. P02b's PR grouped the key table's rows as moves, deletions, and the clipboard, and about ten keys that no test pressed did not show (PageUp and PageDown, Control+Home, Control+Delete, Control+Insert, Shift+Insert, Shift+Delete, Control+Y, Control+Shift+Z, Shift+Home, Shift with the arrows); the review counted the keys the tests pressed and found them (PR #489).
- A rule that gives each item its own look (each clause of a composition its own underline, shortened at its ends) is tested with two neighbours that look the same: the ranges of a text leaf join a range to the one before it when the look is the same (`TextRanges.Add`). P02c's first PR tested clauses that all looked different, and two dotted clauses side by side joined into one underline with no gap; the review found it (PR #523).
- A layer placed over a part with absolute positioning (a placeholder, a caret, an overlay) is tested with a padding on the part: absolute positions start at the parent's padding box, so a layer that ignores the padding is in place only while the padding is 0. P02b's placeholder sat over the field's padding box and showed at the field's corner once the todo sample gave the field a padding; the golden image showed it (PR #492).
- When a design statement changes, search `docs/`, the READMEs, `.cursor/rules/`, and XML docs for every restatement and update them in the same PR.
- A shape or a paint that the design matches to a specification (CSS, a platform's drawing) is tested, and compared in the scene the owner judges, at the specification's corner cases: a translucent value of each paint over another, a value past its range, and the input where the cases of a formula meet. In R4a the CSS comparison scene had an opaque border and radii under half a side; the review found that a translucent border hid the fill under it and that radii summing past a side were not scaled as CSS 5.5 scales them. The first fix took the corner whose square holds the pixel and missed the second arc where the squares of two opposite corners overlap (a lens); a test now draws that lens (PR #248).
- Before a PR says "every X does Y" (every backend implements `IGpuSync`, every Primitive exposes a role), search the whole solution for X. The PR's test plan names the search and the exceptions.

```text
BAD:  "children are disposed in LIFO order, then the scope's own cleanups in LIFO order"
      → test checks that every cleanup ran
GOOD: register cleanups that append to a log in two children and the parent; assert the exact log order
```
